# CODEOWNERS — the file-ownership table from plans/00-shared/conventions.md section 7,
# expressed in a form GitHub can enforce.
#
# OWNER: Dev D (tools/ and CI). Changes to this file need the same PR + 2 approvals as any
# other shared contract.
#
# =========================================================================================
# THE FOUR HANDLES, and how they were established.
#
#   @Sagitoaz   -> Dev A, Unity client
#   @MinhToan4  -> Dev B, Transport
#   @Nghaiz     -> Dev C, Replication
#   @ngtukien   -> Dev D, Master server + tools/CI
#
# Mapped from merged-PR authorship rather than from anybody's say-so, because a wrong handle
# here fails silently: GitHub shows "Unknown owner" and assigns nobody, which is exactly the
# state this file sat in from its creation until 2026-08-15. The evidence is one-to-one —
# #47/#44/#42/#33 (client) are Sagitoaz, #39/#25 (dev b transport) are MinhToan4,
# #35/#28 (dev d master) are ngtukien, and the replication/protocol series is Nghaiz.
#
# All four are repository collaborators, so all four have the WRITE access that code-owner
# review requests require. The second requirement is a branch-protection setting rather than
# anything this file can express: "Require review from Code Owners" must be on in the rule
# for main and develop (docs/branch-protection.md).
# =========================================================================================
#
# HOW GITHUB READS THIS FILE: the LAST matching pattern wins — the opposite of .gitignore.
# So the broad rules come first and the narrow exceptions come after them. Reordering the
# blocks below silently changes who owns what.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default — anything not claimed below reaches all four, so nothing merges unreviewed.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*                                               @Sagitoaz @MinhToan4 @Nghaiz @ngtukien

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dev A — Unity client
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/Ironfront_Reborn/                              @Sagitoaz
/plans/dev-a-unity-client/                      @Sagitoaz

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dev B — Transport
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/Ironfront.Net.Transport/                       @MinhToan4
/plans/dev-b-transport/                         @MinhToan4

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dev C — Replication and simulation
#
# Server-side and shared client scripts live inside A's Unity folder but belong to C, so
# these must come AFTER the /Ironfront_Reborn/ line above to override it.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/Ironfront.Net.Replication/                     @Nghaiz
/plans/dev-c-replication/                       @Nghaiz
/Ironfront_Reborn/Assets/Scripts/Net/Server/    @Nghaiz
/Ironfront_Reborn/Assets/Scripts/Net/Shared/    @Nghaiz

# MovementSimulation is the single shared source of truth for client and server prediction.
# conventions.md marks it "Nobody else may edit" without qualification — it gets its own
# line so a reviewer sees the ownership on the file itself, not on its folder.
/Ironfront_Reborn/Assets/Scripts/Net/Shared/MovementSimulation.cs   @Nghaiz

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dev B again — the implementer/verifier seam, the most important line in this file.
#
# B implements the bit-packing; C writes the conformance tests that verify it. Serialization/
# sits inside C's project and must therefore be re-claimed for B after the block above.
# If the same person could approve both sides, the conformance suite would only prove the
# code agrees with itself instead of agreeing with the spec.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/Ironfront.Net.Replication/Serialization/       @MinhToan4

# ...and the referee side of that same seam belongs to C.
/Ironfront.Net.Protocol.Tests/Conformance/      @Nghaiz

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Dev D — Master server, load-test harness, tooling and CI
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/Ironfront.MasterServer/                        @ngtukien
/Ironfront.Tools.LoadTest/                      @ngtukien
/plans/dev-d-master-server/                     @ngtukien
/tools/                                         @ngtukien
/.github/                                       @ngtukien

# tools/run-integration.ps1 and its scenarios are C's, per conventions.md section 7 — after
# the /tools/ line so it wins.
/tools/run-integration.ps1                      @Nghaiz

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared contracts — PR + 2 approvals (conventions.md sections 2 and 7).
#
# Listing all four handles makes GitHub request all four reviews. The "2 approvals including
# the affected person" rule from the protocol-change process is a branch-protection setting
# (required approving reviews = 2), not something CODEOWNERS can express on its own.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
/Ironfront.Net.Protocol/                        @Sagitoaz @MinhToan4 @Nghaiz @ngtukien
/plans/00-shared/                               @Sagitoaz @MinhToan4 @Nghaiz @ngtukien
